Inheritance
    Inherit the field and behaviour of the parent class to child class is called Inheritance.
    Analogy: A child inherits features from parents (surname, eye color).
In Java-
    A child class gets the parent’s fields and methods for free, so you don’t rewrite code.

    by using keyword "extends"
    Terms to know:
    Parent = superclass = base class
    Child = subclass = derived class
    extends = the keyword that creates the link

What happens in memory
    When you write new Dog(), one single object is created on the heap.
    It contains the parent’s fields (name) plus the child’s fields.
    There is no separate Animal object inside it.

    Heap:  [ Dog object: name (from Animal) + Dog's own fields ]
    Stack: d  ───────────►  (points to that Dog object)

Types of inheritance in Java

Type	                Example	                            Supported?
Single	                B extends A	                            ✅
Multilevel	            C extends B, B extends A	            ✅
Hierarchical	        B and C both extend A	                ✅
Multiple (classes)	    C extends A, B	                        ❌
Hybrid	                mix of the above                ✅ only via interfaces

Why no multiple inheritance? (Diamond problem)
    If class C extended both A and B, and both had a show() method,
    which one would C inherit? That ambiguity is the diamond problem.
    Java avoids it for classes. A class can implement multiple interfaces instead.


super(...) calls the parent constructor
    Key rules:

    - Parent constructor always runs first, then the child. The parent part of the
    object must be ready before the child builds on it.
    - If you don’t write super(...), Java silently inserts super(); (the no-argument call).
    - Trap: if the parent has only a parameterized constructor and no no-arg one, the child
    gets a compile error unless it calls super(args) explicitly.

The Object class
    Every class secretly extends Object, so all objects get these methods:
Method	        Purpose
toString()	    String form of the object
equals()	    Compares objects (default is reference comparison)
hashCode()	    Number used by HashMap, HashSet
getClass()	    Returns runtime class info

Printing an object without overriding toString() gives something like Dog@1b6d3586,
    which is the Object version.

HAS-A (composition)
    class Engine { void start() { } }
    class Car {
        Engine engine = new Engine();   // Car HAS-A Engine
        void drive() { engine.start(); }
    }
Rule of thumb: Use inheritance for IS-A and composition for HAS-A.
When unsure, choose composition (“favor composition over inheritance”),
because it is more flexible and doesn’t lock you into a rigid hierarchy.

Rules summary
    - Private members exist inside the child object but can’t be accessed directly.
        Use getters or setters.
    - Constructors are not inherited (but they are called using super).
    - final class can’t be extended. final method can’t be overridden.
    - Static methods aren’t overridden. They are hidden.
    - A class can extend only one class but implement many interfaces.